    Section(
      title: '+ Explain opioid-induced hyperalgesia (OIH). How do you distinguish it from tolerance? Interpret the opioid risk screening tools and urine drug screen.',
      content:
          '**Opioid-Induced Hyperalgesia (OIH)**:\n- **Definition**: A paradoxical state in which chronic opioid use leads to **increased pain sensitivity** — the opioids are actually making the pain worse.\n- **Mechanism**: Involves central sensitization via NMDA receptor activation, enhanced descending facilitation, and neuroplastic changes in pain-processing pathways.\n- **Clinical Features**: Diffuse pain that is MORE widespread than the original pain pattern, pain that worsens with dose increases (opposite of what tolerance would predict), allodynia (pain from normally non-painful stimuli), hyperalgesia to experimental pain stimuli.\n\n**OIH vs. Tolerance**:\n| **Feature** | **Tolerance** | **OIH** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Pain character | Same pain, same location | Pain MORE widespread, may be different quality |\n| Response to dose increase | Pain temporarily improves | Pain worsens or no improvement |\n| Response to dose decrease | Pain worsens | Pain may actually IMPROVE |\n| Mechanism | Receptor downregulation | Central sensitization |\n| Treatment | Dose increase (short-term) or rotation | Dose reduction, NMDA antagonists, opioid rotation |\n\n**This patient likely has a component of OIH**: Her pain has worsened over the past year despite dose increases, and her pain pattern is more diffuse than expected for her surgical anatomy.\n\n**Opioid Risk Screening Interpretation**:\n- **ORT Score 4 (Moderate Risk)**: Risk factors include female gender, age 45-65, history of depression/anxiety. Moderate risk warrants structured monitoring, not necessarily contraindication to opioids.\n- **COMM Score 8 (Below threshold)**: Current behavior does not suggest active opioid misuse. She takes medications as prescribed, no early refills, no lost prescriptions.\n- **SOAPP-R (Screener and Opioid Assessment for Patients with Pain)**: An alternative screening tool — 14-item questionnaire, score ≥18 suggests higher risk for aberrant behavior.\n\n**Urine Drug Screen (UDS) Interpretation**:\n- Order a **comprehensive UDS with confirmation testing** (immunoassay alone has limitations)\n- Expect: Positive for morphine (prescribed). May be positive for hydromorphone (minor metabolite of morphine — does NOT indicate hydromorphone use).\n- Assess for: Presence of non-prescribed substances (benzodiazepines, illicit drugs), absence of prescribed medication (suggests diversion)\n- **Important**: Natural opioids (morphine, codeine) and synthetic opioids (fentanyl, methadone) require DIFFERENT assay panels. Standard immunoassay may miss fentanyl and methadone.\n- Consider checking the **Prescription Drug Monitoring Program (PDMP)** for multiple prescribers or pharmacies.',
