    Section(
      title: '+ What adjuvant medications and non-pharmacologic treatments should be initiated BEFORE or DURING the taper?',
      content:
          '**Pre-Taper Optimization Strategy** — "Build the floor before removing the ceiling":\n\nThe key principle is to establish effective non-opioid pain management BEFORE beginning the taper, so the patient has alternative tools when opioid doses are reduced.\n\n**Pharmacologic Adjuvants**:\n1. **Duloxetine 60 mg daily**: SNRI with FDA approval for chronic musculoskeletal pain AND comorbid depression/anxiety. Start at 30 mg x 1 week, then increase. Dual benefit — addresses her PHQ-9 of 11 and GAD-7 of 12 simultaneously.\n2. **Gabapentin 300 mg TID** (titrate to 600 mg TID): For neuropathic component (left L5 radiculopathy). Start low, titrate slowly. Caution: may cause sedation/dizziness — coordinate timing with opioid taper.\n3. **Acetaminophen 650 mg TID**: Safe, effective baseline analgesic often overlooked in patients on opioids.\n4. **Topical NSAIDs or lidocaine patches**: For localized back pain without systemic side effects.\n5. **Muscle relaxant optimization**: Replace cyclobenzaprine (anticholinergic, sedating, contributes to fatigue) with tizanidine 4 mg at bedtime or baclofen if muscle spasm component.\n\n**Non-Pharmacologic Interventions**:\n6. **Cognitive Behavioral Therapy for Chronic Pain (CBT-CP)**: Gold standard psychological intervention. Addresses catastrophizing, fear-avoidance, and develops active coping strategies. Weekly sessions for 8-12 weeks. Critical for taper success.\n7. **Physical Therapy**: Reactivation program focusing on core stabilization, graduated aerobic exercise, flexibility. Address fear-avoidance of movement.\n8. **Mindfulness-Based Stress Reduction (MBSR)**: 8-week program shown to reduce pain intensity and improve function in chronic pain. Complements CBT.\n9. **Yoga/Tai Chi**: Evidence-based for chronic low back pain. Improves function, reduces pain, promotes self-efficacy.\n10. **Interventional procedures**: Consider medial branch blocks/radiofrequency ablation for facet-mediated pain, or updated epidural injection if radicular component is significant.\n\n**Address Contributing Factors**:\n11. **Sleep optimization**: Improve trazodone dose or switch to low-dose amitriptyline 10-25 mg (also helps neuropathic pain). Address sleep hygiene.\n12. **Vitamin D supplementation**: 2000-4000 IU daily (level 22 — insufficiency contributes to pain).\n13. **Endocrinology referral**: Low-normal testosterone may represent opioid-induced endocrinopathy. Monitor for improvement with taper.\n14. **Weight management**: Dietitian referral; exercise program. Excess weight worsens back pain.\n\n**Timeline**: Initiate adjuvant treatments and allow 4-6 weeks for therapeutic effect before beginning opioid taper.',
