    Section(
      title: '+ Discuss the role of the Prescription Drug Monitoring Program (PDMP), naloxone co-prescription, and criteria for addiction medicine referral.',
      content:
          '**Prescription Drug Monitoring Program (PDMP)**:\n1. **Mandatory checking**: Most states now require PDMP review before prescribing opioids and at regular intervals during treatment (typically every 1-3 months).\n2. **What to look for**: Multiple prescribers, multiple pharmacies, overlapping opioid prescriptions, concurrent benzodiazepine prescriptions, escalating quantities.\n3. **This patient**: PDMP review should confirm single prescriber, single pharmacy, consistent fill patterns. Any anomalies warrant direct conversation with the patient.\n4. **Documentation**: Note PDMP review in each clinic visit note.\n\n**Naloxone Co-Prescription**:\n- **CDC recommends naloxone** for all patients receiving ≥50 MME/day (this patient is at 120+ MME/day — well above threshold)\n- Prescribe **naloxone 4 mg intranasal (Narcan)** or 0.4 mg auto-injector (Evzio) to keep at home\n- **Educate the patient AND a household contact** on signs of overdose (unresponsive, slow/absent breathing, cyanosis) and naloxone administration\n- Naloxone co-prescription does NOT imply addiction — frame as safety measure analogous to an EpiPen for allergies\n- During the taper, overdose risk is actually INCREASED if the patient relapses to her previous dose after a period of reduced tolerance\n\n**Addiction Medicine Referral Criteria**:\nRefer to addiction medicine or a substance use disorder specialist if:\n1. **Suspected opioid use disorder (OUD)**: Using DSM-5 criteria — look for loss of control (taking more than prescribed), craving, failure to fulfill obligations, continued use despite harm, withdrawal avoidance behavior\n2. **Failed structured taper**: If the patient is unable to reduce despite adequate support, non-opioid optimization, and behavioral treatment\n3. **Concurrent substance use**: Discovery of non-prescribed substances on UDS, alcohol escalation, illicit drug use\n4. **Medication-assisted treatment (MAT) consideration**: Buprenorphine/naloxone (Suboxone) may be appropriate if OUD is diagnosed — provides partial opioid agonism for pain AND treats addiction simultaneously\n5. **History of overdose** or escalating dangerous behavior\n\n**This patient currently does NOT meet criteria for addiction medicine referral**: COMM score below threshold, no aberrant behaviors, motivated for taper. However, maintain vigilance throughout the process.\n\n**Coordination with Other Providers**:\n- PCP: Inform of taper plan; ensure no duplicate opioid prescriptions\n- Pharmacist: Single designated pharmacy; pharmacist can assist with monitoring\n- Mental health: CBT therapist and psychiatrist should be aware of taper timeline to provide concurrent support\n- Emergency department: Provide the patient with a letter outlining her taper plan to prevent ER opioid prescriptions for pain flares',
