    Section(
      title: '+ How would you manage this crisis and support the patient through a difficult phase of the taper?',
      content:
          '**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Acknowledge the distress and take it seriously**:\n\'I can hear how much you are struggling right now, and I take that very seriously. You are not being dramatic, and this is not in your head. What you are experiencing — the increased pain, the poor sleep — these are real physiological responses to the dose change, and they are temporary.\'\n\n**2. Normalize the experience**:\n\'Almost every patient who goes through an opioid taper hits a rough patch somewhere in the process. This does not mean the taper has failed. It means your body is adjusting, and this phase will pass. The fact that you have already reduced from 165 mg to 90 mg is remarkable progress — you have cut your dose nearly in half, and you were still functioning well until this most recent step.\'\n\n**3. Provide a concrete plan (not just reassurance)**:\n\'Here is what I would like to do right now. First, we are going to pause the taper at your current dose. We will NOT go back up, but we will hold steady at 90 mg until your body has had a chance to equilibrate. Sometimes we need to sit at a dose for 4-6 weeks instead of 2 weeks before the next reduction. This is not a failure — this is how individualized tapering works.\'\n\n**4. Address the specific symptoms**:\n\'For the sleep, I am going to increase your trazodone to 100 mg at bedtime tonight and add melatonin 5 mg. For the pain flare, let us add ibuprofen 400 mg three times daily with food for the next 1-2 weeks, and I want you to do the relaxation exercise from your CBT homework before bed. For the anxiety component, we can add clonidine 0.1 mg at bedtime, which will help with both sleep and some of the physical withdrawal symptoms.\'\n\n**5. Reframe the fear of going backward**:\n\'You said you want to go back to your old dose. I understand the temptation, but I want to remind you of something: at your old dose, your pain was a 6 and getting worse. You had constipation, fatigue, low energy, and your pain was not well controlled. The old dose was not working — that is why we started this process. Going back would mean returning to all of those problems.\'\n\n**6. Reinforce accomplishment and commitment**:\n\'You have already done the hardest part. Cutting from 165 to 90 mg is a 45% reduction, and you did that. Your body is stronger than you think. I am not going anywhere — we will get through this difficult phase together. Can we schedule a follow-up visit in 3 days so I can check in with you in person?\'\n\n**7. Safety check**:\n\'One more thing — I always want to ask: are you having any thoughts of hurting yourself or feeling hopeless? It is important that I know, and there is no judgment if you are.\'\n\n**Key principles**: Take the crisis seriously, normalize the experience, provide immediate symptom management, pause (do not reverse) the taper, reframe the desire to go back, reinforce progress, schedule close follow-up, safety screening for suicidality.',
